A class in Java is a blueprint that defines the data and behavior of objects. It can contain fields, methods, constructors, nested types, and initialization blocks.Objects are created as instances of classes.Classes support important OOP concepts such as encapsulation, inheritance, and abstraction.Declaring a Class in JavaA basic class declaration contains the class keyword followed by the class name and class body.class ClassName {    // Fields    // Constructors    // Methods}A class can contain:FieldsMethodsConstructorsInitialization blocksNested classesNested interfacesEnums and records as nested types
            Java

    class Student {
    int id;
    String name;

    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Student student = new Student();

        System.out.println(student.id);
        System.out.println(student.name);
    }
}

Output0
nullExplanation: Here, Student is a class and student is an object of that class. Because the fields are not explicitly initialized, Java assigns their default values.Types of Classes in JavaClasses are commonly described based on their purpose and modifiers. Some important types are:Note: These categories are not mutually exclusive. For example, a POJO can also be a final class, and a concrete class can also be a nested class.1. Final ClassA class declared using the final keyword is called a final class.The main purpose of final on a class is to prevent inheritance.final class Vehicle {}Another class cannot extend it:class Car extends Vehicle {}The above code causes a compilation error because Vehicle is final.Important Point: Declaring a class final does not make its objects immutable.For example:final class Student {    String name;    void setName(String name) {        this.name = name;    }}Although Student cannot be extended, its name field can still be changed.Therefore:final class cannot be subclassed.final method cannot be overridden.final variable cannot be reassigned after initialization.Immutability is a separate design concept. A truly immutable class must prevent its observable state from changing after object creation.
            Java

    final class Base {
    void display()
    {
        System.out.println("Method of Base class");
    }
}

class GFG {
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {
        Base obj = new Base();
        obj.display();
    }
}

OutputMethod of Base class2. Static Nested ClassJava does not allow a top-level class to be declared static.However, a class declared inside another class can be declared static. Such a class is called a static nested class.class Outer {    static class Nested {    }}Unlike a non-static inner class, a static nested class does not require an instance of the outer class.Important PointsOnly nested classes can be declared static.A static nested class can contain both static and instance members.Objects of a static nested class can be created.It cannot directly access non-static members of the outer class without an outer-class object.
            Java

    class Outer {

    static int value = 100;

    static class Nested {

        int number = 10;

        void display()
        {
            System.out.println("Value: " + value);
            System.out.println("Number: " + number);
        }
    }
}

class GFG {
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {

        Outer.Nested obj = new Outer.Nested();
        obj.display();
    }
}

OutputValue: 100Number: 103. Abstract ClassA class declared using the abstract keyword is called an abstract class.An abstract class:Cannot be instantiated directly.Can contain abstract methods.Can contain concrete methods.Can have constructors.Can contain static methods and fields.Can contain final methods.Can implement interfaces.An abstract class does not need to contain an abstract method.
            Java

    interface X {
    int product(int x, int y);
}

abstract class Product implements X {

    public int product(int x, int y) { return x * y; }
}

class GFG extends Product {

    public static void main(String[] args)
    {

        GFG obj = new GFG();

        int result = obj.product(20, 10);

        System.out.println("Product: " + result);
    }
}

OutputProduct: 2004. Concrete ClassA concrete class is a class that can be instantiated and does not leave any required abstract methods unimplemented.A concrete class can:Have fields and methods.Extend another concrete class.Extend an abstract class after implementing the required abstract methods.Implement one or more interfaces.
            Java

    class Calculator {

    int sum(int x, int y) { return x + y; }
}

class GFG {
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {

        Calculator obj = new Calculator();

        System.out.println("Sum: " + obj.sum(10, 8));
    }
}

OutputSum: 185. POJO ClassPOJO stands for Plain Old Java Object. It is a simple Java class used to represent application data without depending on any specific framework.A POJO may contain:FieldsConstructorsGetter and setter methodsOther application-specific methodsGetters, setters, private fields, or a no-argument constructor are not mandatory for a class to be considered a POJO.
            Java

    class Student {

    private int id;
    private String name;

    Student(int id, String name)
    {
        this.id = id;
        this.name = name;
    }

    public int getId() { return id; }

    public String getName() { return name; }
}

class GFG {
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {

        Student student = new Student(101, "Rahul");

        System.out.println(student.getId());
        System.out.println(student.getName());
    }
}

Output101Rahul6. Singleton ClassA Singleton class is designed so that an application normally uses only one instance of that class while providing a controlled way to access it.A typical Singleton implementation contains:A private constructor.A private static reference to the instance.A public static method for obtaining the instance.
            Java

    class Singleton {

    private static Singleton instance;

    private Singleton() {}

    public static Singleton getInstance()
    {

        if (instance == null) {
            instance = new Singleton();
        }

        return instance;
    }
}

class GFG {
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {

        Singleton obj1 = Singleton.getInstance();
        Singleton obj2 = Singleton.getInstance();

        System.out.println(obj1 == obj2);
    }
}

Outputtrue7. Nested and Inner ClassesJava allows one class to be declared inside another class. Such classes are generally called nested classes.Nested classes are broadly divided into:Static nested classesInner classesAn inner class is specifically a non-static nested class.Inner classes can be further classified as:Member inner classLocal inner classAnonymous inner classTherefore, a static nested class should not technically be described as an inner class.A. Member Inner ClassA non-static class declared directly inside another class is called a member inner class.It can access the members of its enclosing object, including private members.
            Java

    class Outer {

    private int value = 100;

    class Inner {

        void show()
        {
            System.out.println("Value: " + value);
        }
    }
}

class GFG {
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {

        Outer outer = new Outer();
        Outer.Inner inner = outer.new Inner();

        inner.show();
    }
}

OutputValue: 100B. Anonymous Inner ClassAn anonymous inner class is an unnamed class declared and instantiated in a single expression.It is commonly used when a small one-time implementation of a class or interface is required.Example 1: Using a Class
            Java

    class Parent {

    void show() { System.out.println("Parent method"); }
}

class GFG {

    public static void main(String[] args)
    {

        Parent obj = new Parent() {
            @Override void show()
            {
                System.out.println(
                    "Anonymous class method");
            }
        };

        obj.show();
    }
}

OutputAnonymous class methodExample 2: Using an Interface
            Java

    interface Hello {
    void show();
}

class GFG {

    public static void main(String[] args)
    {

        Hello obj = new Hello() {
            public void show()
            {
                System.out.println("Anonymous class");
            }
        };

        obj.show();
    }
}

OutputAnonymous classC. Static Nested ClassA class declared static inside another class is a static nested class.
            Java

    class Outer {

    static int data = 100;

    static class Nested {

        void show() { System.out.println("Data: " + data); }
    }
}

class GFG {
    public static void main(String[] args)
    {

        Outer.Nested obj = new Outer.Nested();
        obj.show();
    }
}

OutputData: 100D. Local Inner ClassA class declared inside a block, commonly inside a method, is called a local class or local inner class when it is non-static.
            Java

    class Outer {

    void outerMethod() {

        System.out.println("Outer Method");

        class Inner {

            void innerMethod() {
                System.out.println("Inner Method");
            }
        }

        Inner obj = new Inner();
        obj.innerMethod();
    }
}

class GFG {

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Outer obj = new Outer();
        obj.outerMethod();
    }
}

OutputOuter MethodInner Method
